# Enter the Binary Genealogy of Numbers

A surreal number is created as the simplest value between earlier left and right options. In the finite opening of this construction, binary subdivision produces the dyadic rationals $m/2^n$. Begin with the exact birthday staircase.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

The canonical unit $2^{-n}$ is born on day $n+1$: every unit arrives finitely, but no finite day contains all of them. The paired visualization places linear birthday growth beside exponential decay.

{{visualization:0}}

## Equality becomes integer arithmetic

The central theorem states that $m_1/2^{n_1}$ and $m_2/2^{n_2}$ have the same canonical surreal value exactly when $m_1 2^{n_2}=m_2 2^{n_1}$. Try it, then inspect the decision procedure.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the equality theorem works</summary>
Write both fractions over $2^{n_1+n_2}$. Equality becomes $(m_1 2^{n_2}-m_2 2^{n_1})2^{-(n_1+n_2)}=0$. The binary unit is positive, and a nonzero integer multiple of it cannot vanish, so the coefficient must be zero.
</details>

## Exact arithmetic and canonical forms

Reducing a dyadic means removing factors of two from its numerator. The resulting numerator is odd unless the value is zero, and cross multiplication proves this reduced presentation unique.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

The grids below show the geometric meaning of denominator depth: each level retains previous points and inserts midpoints.

{{visualization:1}}

## Where field arithmetic breaks

The dyadics form a ring, but not a field. If $3$ possessed an inverse $m/2^n$, then $3m=2^n$. Yet powers of two are never divisible by three.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the modular proof</summary>
Modulo $3$, multiplication by $2$ swaps residues $1$ and $2$. Starting from $2^0\equiv1$, the powers therefore alternate forever between $1$ and $2$ and never reach $0$. Thus $3
mid2^n$ for every $n$.
</details>

## Explore further

For background, visit [Surreal numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surreal_number), [Dyadic rationals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyadic_rational), and [Localization of a ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Localization_(commutative_algebra)). The decisive conceptual lesson is to distinguish a birthday cutoff from the ring or field it generates: constructive depth and algebraic closure are different operations.

